## 6. DUPLICATE DETECTION AND DATA LEAKAGE PREVENTION

Implement multiple levels of duplicate detection.

### 6.1 Exact duplicates

Use SHA-256 or another approved cryptographic digest to detect identical files.

### 6.2 Transformed duplicates

Different files may represent the same audio after:

- Resampling.
- Re-encoding.
- Channel conversion.
- Gain adjustment.
- Silence removal.
- Segmentation.

Use an optional audio fingerprinting stage to identify probable duplicates.

Fingerprint similarity is not definitive proof of duplication.

Require review or a deterministic confirmation rule before deleting or merging records.

### 6.3 Near-duplicate transcripts

Detect:

- Repeated transcript text.
- Repeated recordings from the same session.
- Overlapping segments.
- Cloned or copied speech.
- Repeated test samples across dataset releases.

### 6.4 Split leakage prevention

The most important rule:

Do not split a recording into clips and then randomly assign clips to train, validation, and test partitions when they share a speaker or recording session.

Perform split assignment at the appropriate group level.

Use, as applicable:

- Speaker-disjoint partitions.
- Session-disjoint partitions.
- Recording-disjoint partitions.
- Source-corpus separation.
- Organization or contributor separation where required.

A single speaker must not accidentally leak across partitions when the evaluation protocol requires speaker independence.

Maintain an explicit record of all known split-group relationships.

### 6.5 Leakage audit

Every dataset release must run an automated leakage audit.

Report:

- Exact duplicate overlap.
- Probable near-duplicate overlap.
- Shared speaker overlap.
- Shared session overlap.
- Source recording overlap.
- Transcript duplication.
- Any approved exceptions.

A dataset release cannot be labeled SPEAKER_DISJOINT unless the group-level audit supports that claim.

